note bottom of Kernel
  Captured lookup rejects unowned records
  before any reflective access can invoke
  caller code. Local receipts confirm a fixed
  construction role, not permission to plan
  or authority over a session.
end note

note bottom of Storage
  Intrinsic view checks establish physical
  extent before a bounded private copy.
  Caller aliases cannot mutate retained bytes,
  and the snapshot reveals no backing buffer.
end note

note bottom of Schema
  Direct ASCII productions accept only exact
  canonical fields, ordering and primitive
  domains. Fresh encoded bytes expose neither
  the private snapshot nor its construction
  receipt.
end note

note top of Bytes
  Float64Hex: 26 bytes; Ref: at most 107.
  Ref length: 0..MAX_SAFE_INTEGER.
  Opening: exact canonical literal only.
end note

note bottom of Scalars
  Captured array construction creates dense
  own slots without inherited index callbacks.
  A fresh count record with a null prototype
  blocks an inherited iterator, keeping a fixed
  construction extent without caller callbacks.
end note

note bottom of Primitive
  Primitive reservation accepts only finite
  safe numbers within the fixed construction
  cap. It inspects no caller graph and confers
  neither account identity nor a planning
  permission.
end note

note bottom of Account
  Local accounting binds tickets to one private
  account and retains every reserved allowance.
  Its two fields grant no planning permission,
  model receipt or session authority, so a
  copied record cannot authorize model work.
end note

note bottom of Invoices
  Catalog quotes include primitive calls,
  record stores and structural receipt creation.
  Those allowances need independent review
  and measured bounds, not just tests that
  repeat the same arithmetic.
end note

note bottom of Accounted
  Model closure excludes coordinator code even
  when an import carries types rather than
  runtime values. A matching storage shape
  cannot confer a planning permit or attach
  model and session authority.
end note

note right of Controls
  Complete descriptors use a null prototype
  and declare every data value and flag.
  They block inherited hooks that could change
  the fixed cap or install an accessor while
  the private account is being constructed.
end note

note right of Account
  Captured checks inspect only the known
  meter data descriptor, not a caller graph.
  Observed value or writability changes fail
  before debit or final closure, preserving
  private usage and the permanent failure latch.
end note

note right of AccountingError
  Native class fields define error names as
  own data instead of calling inherited setters.
  Rejection construction therefore cannot
  invoke a prototype name hook that changes
  accounting control flow.
end note

note right of Accounted
  A closed switch invokes the prepaid block
  without allocating an operation closure.
  Bounded cleanup preserves the original error
  even when expired tickets reject, so failure
  latches cannot reopen the private account.
end note

note top of Invoices
  Closed primitive block IDs: length <=17.
  Six blocks only; no caller-unit debit.
end note

note top of Controls
  Initial: 2048 + 512 + 512 = 3072.
  Enter once: permanently reserve next512.
  Operation: quote + envelope1024 + gate512.
  Control terms: measurement/review pending.
end note

note bottom of Tests
  The tests observe allocation extents and
  receipt isolation at construction boundaries.
  They do not establish a planning invoice or
  prove that all decision work fits the
  transaction cap.
end note

note bottom of Ledger
  Kernel accounting prepays entries and closed
  blocks before their effects execute. Private
  owners retain a single cumulative cap;
  meter identity does not authorize a model
  or session.
end note

note bottom of Support
  An opaque draft binds dense Boolean support
  to its originating transaction. Sealing
  freezes the same array after a paid full scan,
  so private metadata cannot conceal a copy.
end note

note bottom of Summary
  Support identity retains the count, extrema
  and birth predicate computed during sealing.
  Constructors reuse that private summary
  without a second full scan or weaker checks.
end note

note top of Scalars
  The finite number encoder derives its own
  hex bytes, avoiding a redundant input scan.
  Private number receipts follow fixed identity;
  unseen values still prepay decoding.
end note

note bottom of Budget
  An isolated settled reduction now fits the
  unchanged cap with complete Boolean support.
  Pending boundaries on one ledger still exhaust
  that cap; numerical tests cannot certify the
  complete planning schedule.
end note

note bottom of MemoryFS
  Captured source and installed bytes feed the
  actual compiler in a read only memory graph.
  Both auditors load from captured byte snapshots,
  which proves loading mechanics but not
  complete production enforcement.
end note

note bottom of Policy
  Models must not acquire the ledger constructor
  or its owner through runtime or type imports.
  Thirteen local rejection cases do not replace
  the unchanged auditors' missing enforcement.
end note

note bottom of Bootstrap
  Source bootstrap rejects runtime image bindings
  before it can issue a production seal.
  The launcher lacks fixed same graph dispatch,
  so captured mechanics cannot establish
  independent authority for genuine planning.
end note

note bottom of Startup
  Startup joins registered artifact bytes to
  references chosen by the trusted host.
  Agreement among supplied records cannot grant
  authority; a valid source seal still gates
  bound models and planning permits.
end note

note bottom of Permits
  Native replicas and real channel receipts
  bind each permit in private coordinator tables.
  Without a valid source seal, that protocol
  cannot prove genuine end to end planning.
end note

note bottom of Public
  The package still exports candidates only;
  features exist as an internal source module.
  Existing five root closure omits the R4 graph,
  so it cannot certify the required amended
  public closure.
end note

note top of Kernel
  Actions have a null prototype so missing
  fields cannot invoke inherited hooks.
  Captured own checks select record keys,
  so inherited table entries cannot redirect
  canonical construction.
end note

note bottom of Emission
  Planning tokens use dense character arrays
  and a single join after bounded decoding.
  Canonical output writes directly into fixed
  bytes or compares them in place, avoiding
  repeated data dependent string copies.
end note

note right of Scope
  Failed ingress retains its original error
  while bounded cleanup attempts close tickets.
  The callback test uses real accounting,
  not an admitted hosted scope, genuine permit
  or production source seal.
end note

note top of Loader
  The loader wraps host values and callback
  arguments in the realm that receives them.
  Direct and indirect constructor regressions
  exercise that boundary, not a general
  JavaScript sandbox or production seal.
end note

note right of Loader
  The loader times actual module invocation,
  not just creation of its wrapper.
  An infinite body regression tests that boundary,
  without proving production startup or planning
  deadlines across the complete graph.
end note

note bottom of Channel
  One undecoded handle gates the next frame;
  retained transport bytes publish no second
  pending frame. Successful decoding releases
  raw storage before exposing a successor,
  so coalesced receipt times never renew.
end note

note bottom of RawStorage
  Preflight counts every arrived delimiter
  before publishing the first frame to a reader.
  End of stream drains complete frames before
  a partial tail fails, while receipt time and
  decoded identity remain unchanged.
end note

note top of RawStorage
  C: completion byte cap; I: message cap.
  Retained channel buffers <= 2*C + I.
  Assembly overlap <= 3*C + I.
  Partial-frame slabs: 64 KiB each.
  One waiter; one undecoded handle.
end note

note left of Loader
  Saved filesystem readers recheck the live
  loading gate before selecting the current
  snapshot. Both read paths reject writable
  flags before target dispatch, so cached
  functions cannot restore native access.
end note

note bottom of Loader
  Realm checks capture their intrinsics before
  loaded code can change admission decisions.
  Buffer aliases and self returns keep identity;
  function metadata filters preserve plain
  record fields rather than dropping them.
end note
@enduml
